UPDATE tp_questions q JOIN tp_exams e ON e.exam_id = q.exam_id SET q.body_text = 'The summer of 1858 is remembered as the year smell overtook legislation. The Thames, carrying the untreated waste of two million Londoners, lay exposed beside the Houses of Parliament at low tide, and a heat wave turned the river into what a newspaper called a pestilential ditch. Members hung curtains soaked in chloride of lime; committees sat with windows open onto the water rather than endure the lobbies. The result was an unusually swift decision. Joseph Bazalgette, an engineer who had spent years arguing for an intercepting system, received funding and authority almost at once. His design captured sewage in great brick conduits along the embankments and carried it by gravity to outfalls far downstream, where the tide diluted the discharge. The engineering was conservative — brick, gravity, wide bore — but its geography was radical, moving waste away from the city''s heart instead of merely hiding it. Commentators credited the sewers with ending cholera; the reasoning was wrong, since the disease travelled in water no nose could detect, yet the death rates did fall.' WHERE e.exam_code = 'TP-PRACTICE-2026-SET08' AND q.order_no = 42;
UPDATE tp_questions q JOIN tp_exams e ON e.exam_id = q.exam_id SET q.body_text = 'The summer of 1858 is remembered as the year smell overtook legislation. The Thames, carrying the untreated waste of two million Londoners, lay exposed beside the Houses of Parliament at low tide, and a heat wave turned the river into what a newspaper called a pestilential ditch. Members hung curtains soaked in chloride of lime; committees sat with windows open onto the water rather than endure the lobbies. The result was an unusually swift decision. Joseph Bazalgette, an engineer who had spent years arguing for an intercepting system, received funding and authority almost at once. His design captured sewage in great brick conduits along the embankments and carried it by gravity to outfalls far downstream, where the tide diluted the discharge. The engineering was conservative — brick, gravity, wide bore — but its geography was radical, moving waste away from the city''s heart instead of merely hiding it. Commentators credited the sewers with ending cholera; the reasoning was wrong, since the disease travelled in water no nose could detect, yet the death rates did fall.' WHERE e.exam_code = 'TP-PRACTICE-2026-SET08' AND q.order_no = 43;
UPDATE tp_questions q JOIN tp_exams e ON e.exam_id = q.exam_id SET q.choices_json = '["It exaggerated a change that never really happened","It rested on correct facts about how the disease travelled","It was mistaken about the cause, yet deaths really did fall after the sewers were built","It was proposed by Bazalgette himself to justify the cost"]', q.body_text = 'The summer of 1858 is remembered as the year smell overtook legislation. The Thames, carrying the untreated waste of two million Londoners, lay exposed beside the Houses of Parliament at low tide, and a heat wave turned the river into what a newspaper called a pestilential ditch. Members hung curtains soaked in chloride of lime; committees sat with windows open onto the water rather than endure the lobbies. The result was an unusually swift decision. Joseph Bazalgette, an engineer who had spent years arguing for an intercepting system, received funding and authority almost at once. His design captured sewage in great brick conduits along the embankments and carried it by gravity to outfalls far downstream, where the tide diluted the discharge. The engineering was conservative — brick, gravity, wide bore — but its geography was radical, moving waste away from the city''s heart instead of merely hiding it. Commentators credited the sewers with ending cholera; the reasoning was wrong, since the disease travelled in water no nose could detect, yet the death rates did fall.', q.explanation = 'The passage calls the reasoning wrong (the disease travelled in water, not smell) while noting the death rates did fall — the option about a mistaken cause but a real decline.' WHERE e.exam_code = 'TP-PRACTICE-2026-SET08' AND q.order_no = 44;
UPDATE tp_questions q JOIN tp_exams e ON e.exam_id = q.exam_id SET q.body_text = 'When a manuscript leaves an editor for its referees, the reviewer''s name is usually kept from the author. The reason is practical: a junior critic who attacks a senior figure''s argument may fear for a career, and a referee who can be identified may soften a judgement to avoid making an enemy. Concealing the reviewer protects candour. Some journals go a step further and conceal the author''s name from the reviewer as well, hoping to shift attention to the claim itself — the same method described in the same paragraph should earn the same verdict whether the paper arrives from a small, little-known laboratory or from the department that trained the referee. Neither layer of secrecy works perfectly. Reviewers often guess who wrote a paper from its citation habits and shared datasets, and an unnamed referee can be careless or unfair without cost. Advocates of open review reply that a signed referee has more at stake and criticises more carefully. What almost nobody defends is dropping review altogether — an editor judging everything alone. Peer review, anonymous or signed, is imperfect justice, chosen over no outside scrutiny at all.', q.explanation = 'The passage gives the reasons for concealing reviewers and authors, then details how both fail and the counterproposal, matching the option about explaining the practice while acknowledging its limits.' WHERE e.exam_code = 'TP-PRACTICE-2026-SET08' AND q.order_no = 48;
UPDATE tp_questions q JOIN tp_exams e ON e.exam_id = q.exam_id SET q.body_text = 'When a manuscript leaves an editor for its referees, the reviewer''s name is usually kept from the author. The reason is practical: a junior critic who attacks a senior figure''s argument may fear for a career, and a referee who can be identified may soften a judgement to avoid making an enemy. Concealing the reviewer protects candour. Some journals go a step further and conceal the author''s name from the reviewer as well, hoping to shift attention to the claim itself — the same method described in the same paragraph should earn the same verdict whether the paper arrives from a small, little-known laboratory or from the department that trained the referee. Neither layer of secrecy works perfectly. Reviewers often guess who wrote a paper from its citation habits and shared datasets, and an unnamed referee can be careless or unfair without cost. Advocates of open review reply that a signed referee has more at stake and criticises more carefully. What almost nobody defends is dropping review altogether — an editor judging everything alone. Peer review, anonymous or signed, is imperfect justice, chosen over no outside scrutiny at all.' WHERE e.exam_code = 'TP-PRACTICE-2026-SET08' AND q.order_no = 49;
UPDATE tp_questions q JOIN tp_exams e ON e.exam_id = q.exam_id SET q.choices_json = '["Its supporters expect a signed referee to criticise with more care","It removes every possibility of guessing who wrote a paper","Most journals have now adopted it in place of blinding","It is the arrangement that most journals used first"]', q.body_text = 'When a manuscript leaves an editor for its referees, the reviewer''s name is usually kept from the author. The reason is practical: a junior critic who attacks a senior figure''s argument may fear for a career, and a referee who can be identified may soften a judgement to avoid making an enemy. Concealing the reviewer protects candour. Some journals go a step further and conceal the author''s name from the reviewer as well, hoping to shift attention to the claim itself — the same method described in the same paragraph should earn the same verdict whether the paper arrives from a small, little-known laboratory or from the department that trained the referee. Neither layer of secrecy works perfectly. Reviewers often guess who wrote a paper from its citation habits and shared datasets, and an unnamed referee can be careless or unfair without cost. Advocates of open review reply that a signed referee has more at stake and criticises more carefully. What almost nobody defends is dropping review altogether — an editor judging everything alone. Peer review, anonymous or signed, is imperfect justice, chosen over no outside scrutiny at all.', q.explanation = 'The passage says proponents argue a signed referee "has more at stake and criticises more carefully", i.e. that signing makes criticism more careful.' WHERE e.exam_code = 'TP-PRACTICE-2026-SET08' AND q.order_no = 50;
